{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"aws-iam","skill_description":"Provides verified corrections for IAM behaviors that AI agents frequently get wrong — policy evaluation edge cases, trust policy gotchas, STS session limits, Organizations quirks, and SAML/MFA specifics. Also provides structured workflows for IAM role management and baseline policy generation from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON. Covers condition operator safety (ForAnyValue/ForAllValues with Null checks), bucket policy deny patterns (VPC endpoint restrictions, org paths), confused deputy protection, and service role creation for AWS services (Glue, CloudTrail, Lambda, ECS, etc.) with aws:SourceAccount/aws:SourceArn trust conditions. Applies when creating IAM roles, writing IAM or bucket policies, generating policies from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON, working with STS, Organizations, or condition operators, or any task needing a service or execution role. Does not cover non-IAM authorization like Cognito user-pool policies or app-level RBAC.\n\n\nServices: iam, sts, organizations\nTasks: deploy, configure, secure, policy-generation, permissions, source-code-to-policy, terraform-plan-to-policy\nPersona: developer, devops, architect, security-engineer\nWorkload: serverless, containers, data-analytics, security\n*","skill_name":"aws-iam"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles","skill_description":"Configures EC2 instances to securely call AWS services by creating and attaching IAM roles via instance profiles, eliminating hardcoded credentials. Use when an EC2 instance needs permissions to access AWS services like S3, DynamoDB, SQS, or CloudWatch through temporary credentials.\n\nServices: ec2, iam\nTasks: configure, secure\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS simplifies IAM role creation and setup in service workflows - AWS","context":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) has introduced a new feature that simplifies the creation and configuration of IAM roles directly within service workflows. This new panel appears when performing console tasks related to role configuration, enabling users to set permissions without switching between browser tabs. IAM roles allow for secure AWS cross-service connections using temporary credentials, eliminating the need for hardcoded access keys. The feature is currently available for use with Amazon EC2, AWS Lambda, Amazon EKS, Amazon ECS, AWS Glue, AWS CloudFormation, AWS Key Management Service, AWS Database Migration Service, AWS Systems Manager, AWS Secrets Manager, and AWS IoT Core in the US East (N. Virginia) Region. The feature will gradually become available for additional AWS services and regions. Users can utilize default policies or the simplified statement builder to customize permissions, streamlining resource setup while maintaining IAM role management functionality","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/03/aws-simplifies-iam-role-creation-and-setup/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"AWS IAM now provides role manager to set up IAM roles automatically - AWS","context":"AWS IAM (Identity and Access Management) has introduced a new capability called role manager. This feature automatically sets up IAM roles for AWS services when they are created in the console. Role manager creates or reuses existing roles with the required permissions. Currently, it supports six AWS services, including AWS Lambda and Amazon EventBridge. When creating an AWS Lambda function, for instance, role manager applies the corresponding AWS-managed template. Roles created via role manager are standard IAM roles that can be controlled by the user, and they can be identified as such in the IAM console. Role manager is available in all AWS Regions except for AWS GovCloud (US) and China Regions. Users can enable or disable role manager at any time and inspect the AWS-managed templates it deploys. Role manager simplifies the process of setting up IAM roles and allows for easier permission management when ready to tighten security","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/08/aws-iam-role-manager/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"ingesting-into-data-lake","skill_description":"Import data into the AWS data lake from S3 files, local uploads, JDBC databases (Oracle, SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, RDS, Aurora), Amazon Redshift, Snowflake, BigQuery, DynamoDB, or existing Glue catalog tables (migration). Default target is S3 Tables; standard Iceberg on a general purpose bucket is supported where S3 Tables is not adopted. Handles one-time loads, recurring pipelines, migrations. Triggers on: import data, load data, ingest, sync database, migrate table, move data to AWS, set up pipeline, ETL, pull from Snowflake, query BigQuery into S3, export DynamoDB, CTAS, convert to Iceberg. Do NOT use for setting up or troubleshooting Glue connections (use connecting-to-data-source), creating empty tables (use creating-data-lake-table), running queries (use querying-data-lake), finding tables by fuzzy name (use finding-data-lake-assets), catalog audit (use exploring-data-catalog), or SaaS platforms like Salesforce, ServiceNow, SAP, MongoDB, Kafka.\n\n\nServices: glue, s3, s3tables, athena, dynamodb\nTasks: deploy, migrate\nPersona: developer, data-engineer\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"ingesting-into-data-lake"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Checking AWS docs on Forecast's IAM role requirements for S3 import jobs."}
